        :UjpWtvaGAqAAahRx,
        :w0n0KxaGAqAAaQ3q ;
    ontouml:description """“The pride of place in this classificatory scheme, according to Aristotle, g 
oes to those entities that are neither said-of nor present-in anything. Such entities, Aristotle says, are primary substances [or essential particulars…] His favorite examples are an individual man and a horse […] So, it is natural to interpret him as thinking that among primary substances are concrete particulars that are members of natural kinds.” Example: Socrates 
(Studtmann)""" ;
    ontouml:isAbstract false ;
    ontouml:isDerived false ;
    ontouml:isPowertype false ;
    ontouml:name "EssentialParticular-PrimarySubstance"@en ;
    ontouml:order "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
    ontouml:project :8evc5z6GAqACBh.w ;
    ontouml:restrictedTo ontouml:functionalComplexNature ;
    ontouml:stereotype ontouml:kind .

:vx.TUraGAqAAZwsz a ontouml:Class ;
    ontouml:description "“Nature [of an Object], according to Aristotle, is an inner principle of change and being at rest… This means that when an entity moves or is at rest according to its nature reference to its nature may serve as an explanation of the event… Natures, then, in a way do double duty: once a nature is operative, neither a further active, nor a further passive capacity needs to be invoked.”" ;
    ontouml:isAbstract false ;
    ontouml:isDerived false ;
    ontouml:isPowertype false ;
    ontouml:name "Nature"@en ;
    ontouml:order "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
    ontouml:project :8evc5z6GAqACBh.w ;
    ontouml:restrictedTo ontouml:functionalComplexNature ;
    ontouml:stereotype ontouml:kind .

:wavCVFaAUcEwZgrL a ontouml:Class ;
    ontouml:description "A possible role of an Assertion, relative to a Syllogism is Conclusion (sumperasma). E.g. C: Socrates is mortal." ;
